WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.880
Access control starts with account creation and administrative roles.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.299
A personal deployment may have one trusted user.

00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:09.760
A team deployment may need separate accounts, administrative permissions,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.160
and clear expectations for who can configure models, manage users, upload documents,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.465
or view shared resources.

00:00:15.465 --> 00:00:17.080
The more people who use the system,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.730
the more important it is to avoid shared accounts and informal access.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.825
Authentication considerations depend on how the service is exposed.

00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:28.240
A local-only deployment has a smaller attack surface than a service reachable across a LAN

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:28.960
or the internet.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.680
LAN access can still be sensitive in shared networks.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.210
Remote access introduces more risk and should be deliberately designed with

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.015
authentication,

00:00:36.015 --> 00:00:40.475
TLS at a high level, reverse proxy configuration, logging, and update discipline.

00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:44.840
Reverse proxies can help centralize TLS, hostnames, routing, and access policy,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.680
but they can also create accidental exposure if misconfigured.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.960
Operators should know which interfaces Open WebUI binds to, which ports are reachable,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.630
and what network paths exist from users to the service.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.320
A private AI workspace should not become public infrastructure by accident.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.240
Least privilege applies to both users and connected services.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.480
Not every user needs administrative access.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.280
Not every workflow needs every model or endpoint.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.850
Applications and users should receive only the access needed for their role.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.585
Controlled exposure protects prompts, uploaded files, compute resources,

00:01:13.585 --> 00:01:16.640
endpoint credentials, and the operational integrity of the deployment.
